From: bigjakk <6015188+bigjakk@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 20 Nov 2026 12:00:00 -0500
Subject: kcc: lean main-thread dispatch for raw-movement (pointer-locked) mouse moves

Stock path of a mouse move in the renderer:
  compositor thread: WidgetInputHandlerManager::DispatchEvent ->
    InputHandlerProxy (kMouseMove -> HandlePointerMove ->
    cc::InputHandler::MouseMoveAt) -> MainThreadEventQueue::HandleEvent ->
    QueueEvent, where the move is coalesced into the queued one
    (QueuedWebInputEvent::FilterNewEvent ->
    WebCoalescedInputEvent::CoalesceWith: WebMouseEvent::Coalesce() adds
    movement_x/y onto the kept event and a clone of every sample is appended
    to the coalesced list);
  main thread, once per main frame (mouse moves are rAF aligned):
    MainThreadEventQueue::DispatchRafAlignedInput -> HandleEventResampling
    -> InputEventPrediction::HandleEvents, then
    WidgetBaseInputHandler::HandleInputEvent ->
    WebFrameWidgetImpl::HandleInputEvent -> PointerLockMouseEvent ->
    PointerLockController::DispatchLockedMouseEvent ->
    EventHandler::HandleTargetedMouseEvent ->
    PointerEventManager::DirectDispatchMousePointerEvent ->
    PointerEventFactory::Create, then MouseEventManager::DispatchMouseEvent.

What that costs for a pointer-locked game that only reads
mousemove.movementX/Y:
  - InputEventPrediction::HandleEvents (input_event_prediction.cc) calls
    UpdatePrediction() for every coalesced sample (Kalman predictor by
    default: three Kalman filter updates per sample) and then
    AddPredictedEvents(), which adds predicted events every
    max(2.5 ms, sample interval) up to 25 ms ahead: up to 10 per dispatched
    move for a 1000 Hz or faster mouse, each with two event clones and one
    InputData allocation.
  - PointerEventFactory::Create (pointer_event_factory.cc) calls
    CreateEventSequence() for the coalesced list and for the predicted
    list of every pointermove, building one PointerEventInit and one
    PointerEvent (both garbage collected) per entry, after
    WebFrameWidgetImpl and PointerEventManager have each copied both lists
    (Vector<WebMouseEvent>, then Vector<WebPointerEvent>).
  So an 8000 Hz mouse at 1000 FPS allocates about 8 + 10 PointerEvent /
  PointerEventInit pairs per frame that nothing reads. The positions the
  predictor works on are those of the hidden cursor, and Blink overwrites
  them with the lock position anyway
  (MouseEvent::SetCoordinatesFromWebPointerProperties).

Change (main_thread_event_queue.cc only): in
MainThreadEventQueue::HandleEventResampling, a kMouseMove whose
WebMouseEvent::is_raw_movement_event is set
  1. is not passed to InputEventPrediction::HandleEvents: no predictor
     update, no predicted events;
  2. has its coalesced list reduced to the event itself
     (QueuedWebInputEvent::DropCoalescedSamples rebuilds the
     WebCoalescedInputEvent from Event() and latency_info(), the layout of
     an event that was never coalesced; skipped when there is only one
     sample).
movementX/Y of the dispatched mousemove and pointermove are unchanged: they
come from the event itself, on which WebMouseEvent::Coalesce() has already
summed the movement of all samples, and the event keeps the newest
timestamp. Visible difference: for these pointermove events
getCoalescedEvents() returns one entry (carrying the full movement) and
getPredictedEvents() returns an empty list.

Why the raw-movement flag: MainThreadEventQueue has no pointer-lock state,
and is_raw_movement_event is existing per-event state that the browser sets
only while pointer lock with unadjustedMovement is held (Windows:
HWNDMessageHandler::OnInputEvent via MouseEvent::DispatcherApi::set_movement;
macOS: unaccelerated movement in web_input_event_builders_mac.mm). Limits
that follow from it:
  - pointer lock without unadjustedMovement is not affected. Linux has no
    unadjusted movement at this tag (WindowTreeHost::
    RequestUnadjustedMovement is not implemented there), so the patch does
    nothing on Linux unless raw-movement events are added there;
  - a few raw-movement moves can be dispatched while Blink is not (or no
    longer) locked, e.g. just after document.exitPointerLock() and before
    the browser stops raw input. Those also get one coalesced entry and no
    predicted events.
Skipping the predictor is safe for later unlocked moves: every predictor
resets itself when the gap to its previous sample exceeds 20 ms
(InputPredictor::kMaxTimeDelta).

Not done: skipping the compositor-thread scrollbar work for these moves.
InputHandlerProxy already knows the lock state (is_pointer_locked_), but
cc::InputHandler::MouseMoveAt only hit-tests when
HasAnimatedScrollbars() is true, i.e. when the page has composited overlay
scrollbars. Overlay scrollbars are off by default on Windows and Linux, so
there the call is a few branches; and HandlePointerMove also drives and
cancels scrollbar thumb drags, which should not change.

Gating: base::Feature KccLeanLockedMouseMove, disabled by default, defined
in the anonymous namespace of main_thread_event_queue.cc and read once.
Renderer process, main thread only (HandleEventResampling is called from
DispatchEvents and DispatchRafAlignedInput). With the feature off
HandleEventResampling behaves as before. Mouse moves without the
raw-movement flag and all other event types are not touched.

A/B: --enable-features=KccLeanLockedMouseMove. Under pointer lock with
unadjustedMovement and a 1000-8000 Hz mouse in motion, compare renderer
main-thread time in InputEventPrediction::HandleEvents and
PointerEventFactory::Create / CreateEventSequence, Oilpan allocation rate
and GC frequency, and frame-time 1% lows. Functional check from the
console: a pointermove listener logging getCoalescedEvents().length,
getPredictedEvents().length and movementX should show N and up to 10 on
stock, 1 and 0 with the feature, and the same movementX/Y sums over the
same physical swipe in both.

Platforms: the file builds on all platforms; it has an effect on Windows
and macOS (see above).

Not verified: nothing was compiled or measured; the allocation counts above
come from reading the code.

Build and check status (2026-11-21)
  - Compiled without any change on Windows in the Electron 45.4.1 DCHECK
    build (dcheck_always_on).
  - test/raw-input-test.js: raw pointermoves with the switch carry a
    one-entry coalesced list and no predicted events (switch off: up to 16
    and 10); movementX/Y still sum to (200,120), the coalesced entries to
    200.
  - Not checked: the allocation and CPU savings (not measured); the macOS
    half (not compiled here).
The "not compiled" / "not verified" notes above were written before the
build; they stay for the caveats they list. Details and raw logs:
patches/electron-45/RESULTS.md and results/.

diff --git a/third_party/blink/renderer/platform/widget/input/main_thread_event_queue.cc b/third_party/blink/renderer/platform/widget/input/main_thread_event_queue.cc
index 7a59c1756266e5f9cae169b70581f3d36ead53a2..7da797f9250e33fa2ed7782b5b95f67273d5375e 100644
--- a/third_party/blink/renderer/platform/widget/input/main_thread_event_queue.cc
+++ b/third_party/blink/renderer/platform/widget/input/main_thread_event_queue.cc
@@ -88,6 +88,22 @@ bool IsGestureScroll(WebInputEvent::Type type) {
   }
 }
 
+// kcc: Lean main-thread dispatch for mouse moves that carry raw device
+// movement. The browser only produces such events while pointer lock with
+// unadjustedMovement is held; the page then reads movementX/Y, which
+// WebMouseEvent::Coalesce() has already accumulated on the event itself. When
+// enabled, these events skip input prediction and are dispatched without
+// their per-sample coalesced list, so Blink does not build one PointerEvent
+// per coalesced sample and per predicted point for every dispatched move.
+BASE_FEATURE(kKccLeanLockedMouseMove, base::FEATURE_DISABLED_BY_DEFAULT);
+
+bool IsLeanLockedMouseMove(const WebInputEvent& event) {
+  static const bool enabled =
+      base::FeatureList::IsEnabled(kKccLeanLockedMouseMove);
+  return enabled && event.GetType() == WebInputEvent::Type::kMouseMove &&
+         static_cast<const WebMouseEvent&>(event).is_raw_movement_event;
+}
+
 }  // namespace
 
 class QueuedWebInputEvent : public MainThreadEventQueueTask {
@@ -291,6 +307,18 @@ class QueuedWebInputEvent : public MainThreadEventQueueTask {
 
   WebCoalescedInputEvent* mutable_coalesced_event() { return event_.get(); }
 
+  // kcc: Replaces the event with a copy whose coalesced list holds only the
+  // event itself, which is what an event that was never coalesced looks like.
+  // The event keeps the movement accumulated by WebMouseEvent::Coalesce() and
+  // the timestamp of the newest sample.
+  void DropCoalescedSamples() {
+    if (event_->CoalescedEventSize() <= 1) {
+      return;
+    }
+    event_ = std::make_unique<WebCoalescedInputEvent>(event_->Event(),
+                                                      event_->latency_info());
+  }
+
   void SetQueuedTimeStamp(base::TimeTicks queued_time) {
     event_->EventPointer()->SetQueuedTimeStamp(queued_time);
   }
@@ -914,6 +942,12 @@ void MainThreadEventQueue::HandleEventResampling(
     base::TimeTicks frame_time) {
   if (item->IsWebInputEvent() && allow_raf_aligned_input_ && event_predictor_) {
     QueuedWebInputEvent* event = static_cast<QueuedWebInputEvent*>(item.get());
+    // kcc: no predictor update, no predicted events and no per-sample
+    // coalesced list for raw-movement (pointer-locked) mouse moves.
+    if (IsLeanLockedMouseMove(event->Event())) {
+      event->DropCoalescedSamples();
+      return;
+    }
     event_predictor_->HandleEvents(*event->mutable_coalesced_event(),
                                    frame_time);
   }
